  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 AM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nThe latest RAP analysis and satellite/radar imagery show high pressure \nstretching across the southern Great Lakes early this morning. Surface \nobservations across northern Wisconsin indicate quiet, fair weather with \ntemperatures dropping into the low 50s to low 60s and calm to \nlight southwest winds. Upstream, weak shortwaves and modest warm \nadvection are generating light showers across central Minnesota \nand northern Iowa. Further west, a potent subtropical ridge is \nbuilding northeast over the Intermountain West while a cold front \nmoves east across Saskatchewan. As this strong upper ridge expands\neastward and the cold front approaches the region, heat and \nperiodic thunderstorm potential will remain the primary focus \nthrough early next week.\n\nHigh pressure will slide to the eastern Great Lakes today, allowing southwest \nflow to increase across the region. Breezier conditions are expected with \ngusts up to 20 mph. Diurnal fair-weather cumulus will develop by late morning \naround 4500 to 5500 feet before dissipation this evening. Highs today will rise \ninto the upper 70s to lower 80s. Clouds will increase tonight as \na cold front advances into northern Minnesota. A few showers and \nelevated thunderstorms will approach north-central Wisconsin after\nmidnight as elevated instability increases to 500-1000 J/kg.\n\nThe cold front will lose definition as it crosses the Upper Peninsula and \nWisconsin on Saturday, with the strongest low-level convergence residing over \nthe Upper Peninsula. Warm, humid air will surge into the area on Saturday as \ninstability increases to 1000-2000 J/kg. Isolated thunderstorms could form \nduring peak heating, though capping in the 800-700mb layer will limit overall \ncoverage. Should storms develop, seasonably strong vertical shear would \nsupport organized cell structures capable of producing large hail and damaging \nwinds. Highs on Saturday will climb into the mid 80s.\n\nOn Sunday and Monday, large-scale ascent driven by warm advection and passing \nshortwaves will bring the highest potential for organized convection. A warm \nfront is forecast to lift northeast into the area by Sunday afternoon, \nproviding a surface focus beneath strong warm advection aloft. An environment \ncharacterized by 2000-3000 J/kg of CAPE, 35-40 knots of deep-layer shear, and \nan upper-level jet streak will be favorable for severe storms. However, capping \nwill remain a significant hindrance as 700mb temperatures rise to near 12C. \nPotential for strong to severe storms will persist into Monday as a shortwave \ntrough and weak cold front push through the state.\n\nIncreasing heat will accompany the mid-level ridge on Sunday and \nMonday. Afternoon highs on Sunday will range from the upper 80s to\nmid 90s, with dewpoints in the upper 60s to lower 70s pushing \nheat index values into the mid-to-upper 90s across central and \neast- central Wisconsin. Heat indices will remain in the mid 90s \non Monday. Following the frontal passage on Monday night, \ntemperatures will remain slightly above seasonal averages through \nTuesday, Wednesday, and Thursday, with high temperatures generally\nreaching the upper 70s to mid 80s. Widespread precipitation \nchances look to remain low through the remainder of the work week \nas high pressure settles back across the region.",
